1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,599
For every token an H100 generates, it waits

2
00:00:01,619 --> 00:00:04,450
on data about 145 times longer than it spends on math.

3
00:00:04,470 --> 00:00:06,574
The setup: gpt-oss-120b,

4
00:00:06,594 --> 00:00:08,960
one GPU, one user.

5
00:00:08,980 --> 00:00:11,255
MLOps engineer @kmeanskaran,

6
00:00:11,275 --> 00:00:13,098
in a long X article on October 1,

7
00:00:13,118 --> 00:00:14,790
worked it out from spec sheets.

8
00:00:14,810 --> 00:00:17,031
How? The math for one token takes

9
00:00:17,051 --> 00:00:18,970
an H100 about 0.01 ms.

10
00:00:18,990 --> 00:00:20,982
The author estimates that each token

11
00:00:21,002 --> 00:00:23,730
means hauling about 5 GB of weights out of memory.

12
00:00:23,750 --> 00:00:26,620
At 3.35 TB/s of memory bandwidth,

13
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:28,930
that haul takes 1.5 ms.

14
00:00:28,950 --> 00:00:30,652
So one user tops out near

15
00:00:30,672 --> 00:00:33,260
670 tokens a second, in theory.

16
00:00:33,280 --> 00:00:35,717
The H200 has the same compute as the H100.

17
00:00:35,737 --> 00:00:39,730
What changed is memory: 141 GB at 4.8 TB/s.

18
00:00:39,750 --> 00:00:41,254
Training is hungrier for memory:

19
00:00:41,274 --> 00:00:43,560
about 18 bytes per parameter.

20
00:00:43,580 --> 00:00:47,536
Rough math: gpt-oss-120b takes about 2.1 TB to train,

21
00:00:47,556 --> 00:00:49,680
but only about 65 GB to serve.

22
00:00:49,700 --> 00:00:52,606
How do 117 billion parameters fit in 65 GB?

23
00:00:52,626 --> 00:00:53,950
4-bit quantization.

24
00:00:53,970 --> 00:00:56,045
In BF16 it would be 234 GB.

25
00:00:56,065 --> 00:00:59,410
Squeezed to 4-bit, it shrinks to about 65 GB.

26
00:00:59,430 --> 00:01:01,030
On Hugging Face's price list,

27
00:01:01,050 --> 00:01:03,660
an A100 80 GB at $2.50 an hour can just about hold it.

28
00:01:03,680 --> 00:01:06,471
Kimi K3's weights come to about 1.56 TB.

29
00:01:06,491 --> 00:01:10,310
Even eight H200s, 1,128 GB in all, can't hold them.

30
00:01:10,330 --> 00:01:12,688
But the author admits these GPUs were never benchmarked.

31
00:01:12,708 --> 00:01:15,170
The speeds are spec-sheet math.

32
00:01:15,190 --> 00:01:16,965
The author's advice is two divisions:

33
00:01:16,985 --> 00:01:19,430
weights divided by VRAM tells you if it fits.

34
00:01:19,450 --> 00:01:21,205
The weights each token must read,

35
00:01:21,225 --> 00:01:23,300
divided by bandwidth, tell you how fast it can go.

36
00:01:23,320 --> 00:01:26,540
Do the two divisions first. Then pay for the card.
